-- title:
--   Cyclic-quotient j reading passes to every algebraically closed overfield
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative ring and $\ell', M', q$ natural numbers with $M'$ nonzero. Assume two variable-change stability hypotheses, for every $A$-algebra $T$: that `IsLevelPStructure` at $\ell'$ (the conditions that the two marked pairs satisfy the affine Weierstrass equation, that their $x$-coordinates are roots of $W.\mathrm{pre}\Psi\,\ell'$, and that the elements $\mathrm{indepElt}$ in both orders are units) is preserved on passing from $W$ and $D$ to $C \bullet W$ and $D.\mathrm{variableChange}\,C$; and that `IsGamma0PowAt` at $(p,k)$ — `IsTwoKernel` when $p^k = 2$, `IsCyclicGenKernel` otherwise — is preserved on passing from $(W,h)$ to $(C \bullet W, \mathrm{kernelVariableChangeDeg}\,C\,(\mathrm{gamma0PowDeg}\,p\,k)\,h)$. Fix group laws $\mathcal{G}$ over $A$ and a level transport $\mathcal{T}$ for $\mathcal{G}$ at $q$, and let $R = \mathrm{rigidDataPow}$ be the rigidification of the product of the prime-power $\Gamma_0(M')$ component, the level-$\ell'$ component and the Drinfeld level-$q$ component. Let $K$ be a field over $A$ in which $M' \ne 0$, let $x_0 \in R.\mathrm{Pt}\,K$, let $y \in K$, and let $d$ be a nonzero natural number dividing $M'$. Suppose given an algebraically closed field $\Omega_0$ over $A$ and over $K$ (compatibly), and a raw datum $x$ over $\Omega_0$ — a Weierstrass curve $x.\mathrm{curve}$ with unit discriminant together with level data and their `IsLevel` witness — whose class in $R.\mathrm{Pt}\,\Omega_0$ is the image of $x_0$ under the base-change map of $R.\mathrm{toLevelModuliDatum}$ along $K \to \Omega_0$, and assume that for every point $g$ of the affine curve $x.\mathrm{curve}$ which is cut out by the $\Gamma_0$-data, in the sense that $g$ has exact additive order $M'$ and that for every prime $p \mid M'$, every $n$ and every nonsingular affine point $(x_1,y_1)$ with $n \cdot g = (x_1,y_1)$ and $\mathrm{addOrderOf}(n \cdot g) = p^{v_p(M')}$ one has that $x_1$ is a root of the polynomial $x.\mathrm{level}.1\,p$, the image of $y$ in $\Omega_0$ equals $\mathrm{cyclicQuotientJ}$ of $x.\mathrm{curve}$ at the subgroup generated by $(M'/d) \cdot g$ with parameter $d$, i.e. $c_4^3/\Delta$ of the corresponding cyclic quotient curve. Then for any algebraically closed field $\Omega$ over $A$ and over $K$ (compatibly), any raw datum $x'$ over $\Omega$ whose class is likewise the image of $x_0$, and any point $g'$ of $x'.\mathrm{curve}$ satisfying the same cut-out condition relative to $x'.\mathrm{level}.1$, the image of $y$ in $\Omega$ equals $\mathrm{cyclicQuotientJ}$ of $x'.\mathrm{curve}$ at the subgroup generated by $(M'/d) \cdot g'$ with parameter $d$.
--
--   This is the independence statement for the reading of a $K$-rational value $y$ as the $j$-invariant of the quotient of the underlying curve by the order-$d$ subgroup cut out by the $\Gamma_0(M')$-data: once the reading holds for one algebraically closed overfield of $K$, one raw representative and all cut-out generators, it holds for every such choice. It is used in the identification of the $q$-expansion value at a full-level Tate point with the cyclic-quotient $j$-invariant in the prime-power $\Gamma_0$ setting.
-- source:
